# D51 / `todo/t0718` — `unify`'s smart-pointer coercion is keyed on the TYPED
# builtin IDENTITY (`DefInfo.builtin_kind`), never on the definition's NAME.
#
# A USER type that happens to be spelled `RWLock` is NOT the builtin `RWLock`: it
# gets its own `DefId` with `builtin_kind = None`, so it is not transparent for
# coercion and this mismatch is a type error.
#
# ⛔ BEFORE THE TYPED FLAG THIS COMPILED, because `unify` asked
# `name == "Mutex" || name == "Shared" || name == "RWLock"` — a question a USER
# declaration answers `true`. So the checker unified `RWLock[int]` with `int` and
# the program went on to build and run. MEASURED at the pre-fix compiler:
# `gg check` OK, C emits `-Wint-conversion`, and the program RUNS and prints a
# raw heap pointer where an `int` was expected (e.g. `187650505388705` — ⚠ A
# POINTER VALUE, DIFFERENT ON EVERY RUN under ASLR; illustration, not a
# reproducible constant).
#
#
# Direction: `int` EXPECTED, builtin-named wrapper supplied (`unify` arm B).
# Asserted by `coercion_identity_user_rwlock_struct_ret_rejects`.

struct RWLock[T]:
    T value

int take_int(int x):
    return x + 1

void main():
    RWLock[int] w = RWLock[int](7)
    print(take_int(w))
